  "summary": "The code contained a critical OS command injection vulnerability and poor architectural practices regarding HTTP method handling. The refactored code enforces strict command whitelisting, uses subprocess for safer execution, and adopts PEP 8 naming conventions.",
  "fixed_code": "import subprocess\nfrom flask import Flask, request, abort\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# Whitelist of allowed commands to prevent arbitrary execution\nALLOWED_COMMANDS = {'status': ['/usr/bin/status_script'], 'restart': ['/usr/bin/restart_service']}\n\n@app.route(\"/command\", methods=['POST'])\ndef execute_command() -> str:\n    '''\n    Receive a command from a POST request, \n    validate against a whitelist, and execute safely.\n    '''\n    action = request.form.get('action')\n    \n    if action not in ALLOWED_COMMANDS:\n        abort(400, description=\"Invalid or missing action\")\n\n    try:\n        subprocess.run(ALLOWED_COMMANDS[action], check=True, shell=False)\n        return \"Command executed successfully\"\n    except subprocess.CalledProcessError:\n        abort(500, description=\"Command execution failed\")"
